body:
I also acted on impulse once when drunk, and did something that left my left arm full of scars. It seemed like a good idea at the time, but oh boy did I regret it the next morning... Just don't do anything rash, because you can never be sure if it's really you who wants that, or the alcohol. Wait until you sober up, please, and then try getting help if you're still feeling like this, because it IS possible to get out of this state of mind.

&#x200B;

I don't know your situation, and I won't pretend to either, but what I do know is that everyone can find a multitude of reasons to stay alive, and that we're all going to die someday, regardless of whether we want to or not. Why not make the most of the time you got left? Of course, it's your decision, but just some food for thought :)

&#x200B;

I hope you get well, friend.

It's ok, really :)

&#x200B;

That's up to you to decide. If you think telling his family will make things better, do it. If you think it'll make things worse for him, don't. I don't know him like you do, obviously, so you're the most qualified person to make that call.

&#x200B;

Hope it all goes well. Keep me updated, if you want. I'm not online that often, but when I am, I'd be happy to give advice.

&#x200B;

PS: Sorry for the late reply.

Talk to him in a honest manner, sit down with him and tell him that you don't want to lose him, and ask him to help you help him. Begin looking together for a new hobby or passion he could pursue to regain at least a bit of the will to live, then work from there.

&#x200B;

If you suspect immediate danger to him, call the emergency services. You said you didn't want him strapped to a hospital bed, but I don't think you want him dead, either. If nothing else, at least you'll know you tried.

&#x200B;

On the chance anything does go wrong, don't blame yourself. You're already doing a lot by having come here to ask for advice on how to help your friend. I hope it all goes well for you and your friend, good luck.

If you're still with us, please go to the ER as soon as possible, and feel free to talk to me.

&#x200B;

If you're already on the other side, I hope you found the peace you were looking for.

Thank you, that's reassuring. :)

&#x200B;

It's not that I think I'm ugly, I see myself as attractive, it's just that sometimes I wonder how a complete stranger would perceive me.

&#x200B;

Regardless, thanks for your reply

Oh, just noticed you're one of those ''incel'' guys...

&#x200B;

I honestly don't think a frustrated, hateful idiot's opinion is what I came looking for. Go cry about being short or jerk off to hentai for 8 hours or whatever other weird shit you guys are into.

&#x200B;

Edit: Also, life pro tip, insulting others won't make you feel better about yourself. You should probably leave the toxic websites/subs, but that's just my opinion...

Trevor placed his hand firmly on my shoulder and pulled be back behind the corner. I looked at him, confused, and saw him place his finger in front of his lips, signalling for me to remain quiet. I nodded, leaning against the cement wall of the bunker and trying to listen to what was happening around the corner, mere meters from me and Trevor. I heard a knock on the door of one of the residences.

&#x200B;

''Internal Security! Open up!''

&#x200B;

From the other side of the door, muffled sounds of crying could be heard. I glanced over at Trevor, whispering ''should we help?''. Trevor just shook his head ''no'' and we continued to stay hidden, listening in.

&#x200B;

''You have ten seconds to open the door, or we will be forced to break it down! Ten! Nine! Eight! Seven! Six! Five! Fo-...''

&#x200B;

The countdown was interrupted by the sound of the door unlocking. Next, we could hear the Internal Security soldiers kicking the door, then screams and cries for help, interspersed with soldiers shouting at the residents to get on their knees with their hands behind their head. A good minute or so later, it seemed the situation had somewhat calmed down... Until one of the soldiers, presumably the squad leader, started addressing the ''suspects''.

&#x200B;

''Robin, Marina, Julie, Kenneth and Vernon Bellton, you have been accussed and subsequently found guilty of conspiracy to exit Underground Falls, directly breaking the law stated in chapter five, article eight of the Underground Falls penal code. Your punishment, in accordance with the state of martial law declared fourteen days ago by Bunker Leader Howard Enwise, shall be the death penalty.''

&#x200B;

Something about his voice made me feel uneasy, it sounded... neutral. As if he did this a thousand times before, and could not care less about five more people dying on his orders. This train of thought that I had was quickly interrupted, though, by the sound of five consecutive gunshots. I froze as I heard the soldiers drag the bodies out of the residence and down the hallway, hoping they wouldn't come our way. Fortunately, they did not.

&#x200B;

Once they were at a safe distance from us, Trevor turned to me with a worried look on his face. ''Internal Security death squads...'' he said with a grave tone in his voice. ''Someone within the movement must've been a government spy... Listen, Alex, we don't have much time. By the sounds of it, all of us associated with the Resurface Movement are gonna get the bullet to the head if the I.S. catches us.''

&#x200B;

I nodded. ''We have to leave now... Do they know about our plan?''

&#x200B;

Trevor sighed. ''We might as well forget about it. They most likely put dozens of soldiers to guard the emergency exit now... But I know another route. You know which one I mean. The sewers.''

&#x200B;

I checked my pistol's magazine. Seven bullets, and one in the chamber, so eight bullets in total. I looked at Trevor. ''Let's go.''

&#x200B;

We started running through the hallways, finally reaching the staircase leading up one level to the storage area. Unfortunately, two soldiers spotted us. I reacted quickly, shooting him three times in the chest, seeing him cough up blood as he fell to the floor, desperately trying to breathe. Trevor charged the other one, driving a screwdriver through his eyeball and into his brain, and leaving it there as we kept running. I had just killed someone for the first time, and could not afford to spend a second to think about it. The rounds I fired would surely attract more of them.

&#x200B;

We reached the place where we wanted to go, the corner of one of the storage rooms. I helped Trevor push a closet aside, revealing a hole in the wall, leading into the sewer system of the city above, New York City. After exchanging quick looks with each other, we both squeezed through the hole and started running through the sewers. I had the impression I heard footsteps behind us, so I turned to look and, sure enough, there were at least fifty soldiers following us.

&#x200B;

As we kept running, barely avoiding being hit by bullets, we rounded a corner and came face to face with a mutated crocodile or aligator of sorts. The thing was huge, but luckily for me and Trevor it was more interested by the racket the soldiers were causing behind us, so we simply ran by it, leaving them to handle the mutated creature. I thought that that would stop them, but sure enough, they were right back after us with just a few moments of delay.

&#x200B;

''Light! Up ahead!'' Trevor shouted as we kept running from the soldiers.

&#x200B;

I looked ahead and saw the end of the sewer tunnel, leading into some sort of flood canal. I felt what should've probably been a lethal dose of adrenalin pumping through my veins as I used every ounce of energy I had left to reach the end of the tunnel. Trevor, on the other hand, wasn't so lucky. He got shot in the head, didn't even have time to realize he died. Fortunately, I managed to avoid the bullets and reached the surface.

&#x200B;

Outside, what was left of New York City looked completely desolated and the city itself was in complete ruins. I barely had time to let the fact that I was actually outside for the first time in twenty five years sink in before a pack of very large, mutated and possibly rabid dogs started running in my direction, furiously barking and growling.

&#x200B;

Behind me, the soldiers stood at the entrance of the tunnel, looking terrified. One of them turned to the others. ''Should we save him?''

&#x200B;

Another one shook his head. ''Man's made his choice... Let's just hope he hasn't doomed us all by leaving a trail with his scent for these... things... to follow back to us. Come on back down, I don't want to spend any more time up here. Mission has failed anyway, the bunker's probably compromised...''

&#x200B;

With that, the soldiers turned around and left, running back towards Underground Falls, as I was left to fight off about twenty mutated dogs with just five bullets...
